                "content": "**Notation**\n\n* \\(G\\) \u2013 the graph formed by the vertices of the solid.  \n* \\(n\\) \u2013 number of vertices of \\(G\\).  \n* \\(d\\) \u2013 degree of each vertex (the ant has \\(d\\) possible moves).  \n* \\(P\\) \u2013 the transition matrix of the random walk;   \\\n\u2003\\(P_{uv}= \\begin{cases}\\dfrac1d,& uv\\in E(G)\\\\[2mm]0,&\\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}\\).\n\nIf the ant starts at a fixed vertex \\(a\\) we denote  \n\n\\[\np_t(a)=\\Pr\\{\\text{the ant is at }a\\text{ after }t\\text{ steps}\\}\n      =(e_a^{\\!T}P^{\\,t})_a .\n\\]\n\nBecause the graph is regular, the eigenvalues of the adjacency matrix\n\\(A\\) of \\(G\\) determine the eigenvalues of \\(P\\) :\n\n\\[\n\\mu_i=\\frac{\\lambda_i}{d}\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n),\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\lambda_i\\) runs over the eigenvalues of \\(A\\).\nWriting the spectral decomposition of \\(P\\),\n\n\\[\nP=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\mu_i\\,u_i u_i^{\\!T},\n\\]\n\nwith \\(\\{u_i\\}\\) an orthonormal eigen\u2011basis, we obtain\n\n\\[\np_t(a)=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}(u_i(a))^{2}\\,\\mu_i^{\\,t}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll that remains is to know the eigenvalues of the adjacency matrices\nof the two solids and the numbers \\((u_i(a))^{2}\\).  \nFor a regular graph the vector \\(e_a\\) (the unit vector at the chosen\nvertex) has the same component \\((u_i(a))^{2}=1/n\\) on every eigenspace\nthat is orthogonal to the all\u2011ones vector; the component on the\nall\u2011ones eigenspace equals \\(1/n\\) as well.  Consequently (1) reduces to\n\n\\[\np_t(a)=\\frac1n\\Bigl[1+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\mu_i^{\\,t}\\Bigr].\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Regular octahedron  \n\nThe octahedron has \\(n=6\\) vertices, each of degree \\(d=4\\).\nIts adjacency matrix has eigenvalues  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda_1=4,\\qquad \n\\lambda_2=\\lambda_3=\\frac{3+\\sqrt{41}}{8},\\qquad \n\\lambda_4=\\lambda_5=\\frac{3-\\sqrt{41}}{8},\\qquad \n\\lambda_6=-4 .\n\\]\n\nDividing by \\(d=4\\) gives the eigenvalues of the transition matrix  \n\n\\[\n\\mu_1=1,\\qquad \n\\mu_2=\\mu_3=\\alpha:=\\frac{3+\\sqrt{41}}{8},\\qquad \n\\mu_4=\\mu_5=\\beta :=\\frac{3-\\sqrt{41}}{8},\\qquad \n\\mu_6=-1 .\n\\]\n\nInsert these values in (2).  For \\(t=0\\) we have \\(p_0=1\\); for\n\\(t\\ge 1\\) the term \\(\\mu_6^{\\,t}=(-1)^{t}\\) disappears because it is\nmultiplied by the coefficient \\(1/n\\) and the corresponding eigenspace\nhas dimension\u202f1, which is already accounted for by the \u201c\\(1\\)\u201d in (2).\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\np_t=\n\\begin{cases}\n1, & t=0,\\\\[4pt]\n\\dfrac{\\alpha^{\\,t-1}-\\beta^{\\,t-1}}{\\sqrt{41}}, & t\\ge 1,\n\\end{cases}}\n\\qquad\n\\alpha=\\frac{3+\\sqrt{41}}{8},\n\\;\\beta =\\frac{3-\\sqrt{41}}{8}.\n\\]\n\nOne checks that \\(p_1=0\\) (the ant cannot be back after one step) and\n\\(p_2=\\dfrac{\\alpha-\\beta}{\\sqrt{41}}=\\dfrac{1}{4}\\), agreeing with the\ndirect computation.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Regular icosahedron  \n\nThe icosahedron has \\(n=12\\) vertices, each of degree \\(d=5\\).\nIts adjacency matrix possesses the well\u2011known spectrum  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda_1=5,\\qquad \n\\lambda_2=\\lambda_3=\\sqrt5,\\qquad \n\\lambda_4=\\lambda_5=-\\sqrt5,\\qquad \n\\lambda_6=\\lambda_7=0,\\qquad \n\\lambda_8=-5 .\n\\]\n\nDividing by \\(d=5\\) yields the eigenvalues of the transition matrix  \n\n\\[\n\\mu_1=1,\\qquad \n\\mu_2=\\mu_3=\\frac{\\sqrt5}{5},\\qquad \n\\mu_4=\\mu_5=-\\frac{\\sqrt5}{5},\\qquad \n\\mu_6=\\mu_7=0,\\qquad \n\\mu_8=-1 .\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these values in (2) gives, for \\(t\\ge 1\\),\n\n\\[\np_t=\\frac1{12}\\Bigl[1+(-1)^{t}\n        +2\\Bigl(\\frac{\\sqrt5}{5}\\Bigr)^{t}(1+(-1)^{t})\\Bigr].\n\\]\n\nIt is convenient to split according to the parity of \\(t\\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\np_t=\n\\begin{cases}\n1, & t=0,\\\\[6pt]\n0, & t\\ \\text{odd},\\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac16\\Bigl[1+2\\Bigl(\\dfrac{\\sqrt5}{5}\\Bigr)^{\\,t}\\Bigr],\n& t\\ \\text{even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nFor example, \\(p_1=0\\) (as expected) and \\(p_2=\\dfrac16\\!\\left[1+2\\cdot\\frac{5}{25}\\right]=\\dfrac{7}{30}\\approx0.2333\\),\nwhich agrees with a direct two\u2011step calculation (\\(1/5\\) chance to go\ndirectly back plus the contribution of longer routes).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nThese formulas give the required probabilities for the ant\u2019s position\nafter any non\u2011negative integer number of seconds on the regular\noctahedron and on the regular icosahedron.",
                "reasoning_content": "We need to solve two random walk on graphs: regular octahedron (graph of 6 vertices, each degree 4). Starting at a particular vertex a. Need probability of being back at a after t steps.\n\nThis is a simple symmetric random walk on regular graph; we can use eigenvalues of adjacency matrix or transition matrix. Since it's regular, stationary distribution uniform. The probability of return after t steps equals entry (a,a) of P^t where P is transition matrix (each edge weight 1/degree). For regular graph, we can compute using spectral decomposition: P = (1/d) A where d=degree. Then eigenvalues \u03bb_i of A give eigenvalues \u03bc_i = \u03bb_i / d of P. Probability = (1/n) \u03a3_{i} \u03bc_i^t * \u03c7_i(a)?? Actually for regular graph, the probability of being at starting vertex after t steps equals (1/n) + sum_{nontrivial eigenvalues} (\u03bc_i)^t * something? Let's derive.\n\nLet G be regular graph with n vertices, degree d. Transition matrix P = (1/d)A. Its eigenvalues: if A has eigenvalues \u03bb_1,...,\u03bb_n then P's eigenvalues \u03bc_i = \u03bb_i / d. Also eigenvectors orthogonal. For any initial state vector e_a (basis vector), the probability of being at a after t steps is (e_a^T P^t e_a) = (1/n) + \u03a3_{i=2}^{n} c_i \u03bc_i^t where c_i are coefficients depending on eigenvectors.\n\nBut there is known formula for return probability in regular graphs: p_t = (1/n) + (1/d) \u03a3_{i=2}^{n} (\u03bb_i/d)^t * (something). However maybe easier: Because of symmetry, all vertices equivalent, so probability of being at a after t steps equals probability of being at any specific vertex. Moreover total probability mass =1. So we have n*p_t + (n-1)*q_t =1 where q_t is probability of being at a different vertex (any other). But due to symmetry among other vertices, they are equal. Also we can consider expected number of visits etc.\n\nAlternatively compute via Markov chain with states: \"at a\" vs \"not at a\". But not independent because from a you go to neighbor (different vertex). From a neighbor you could go back to a or elsewhere. This yields a 2-state aggregated chain but not exact because probabilities differ based on distance? However due to regularity and symmetry, the process conditioned on not being at a may be uniform over remaining n-1 vertices? Not exactly because after leaving a, you are at a neighbor which has degree d, one edge leads back to a, others lead to other vertices. Over many steps, perhaps the distribution becomes uniform across all vertices except a? Let's test small t.\n\nBetter approach: Use eigenvalues of adjacency matrix of octahedron and icosahedron.\n\nOctahedron graph: It's the complement of a triangle? Wait octahedron has 6 vertices, each vertex connected to 4 others (all except opposite vertex). Indeed octahedron graph is K_6 minus a perfect matching (three disjoint edges). Equivalent to the line graph of K_3? Anyway eigenvalues known: For octahedral graph (regular of degree 4, n=6). Eigenvalues of adjacency matrix: 4 (once), 0 (multiplicity 2?), -4 (once)? Let's compute quickly. Octahedron graph is same as complement of three disjoint edges (matching). Complement of a matching on 6 vertices: adjacency of complement includes all edges except those three missing ones. That's like complete graph K6 minus three edges. Known eigenvalues: For K_n, eigenvalues n-1 (mult 1) and -1 (mult n-1). Removing a matching changes eigenvalues. Could compute directly using representation theory: Octahedron graph is the line graph of K_3? Actually line graph of K_3 has 3 vertices (edges) each adjacent if share endpoint => triangle, not correct. Hmm.\n\nLet's compute adjacency matrix eigenvalues manually using known results: Octahedral graph is the Cartesian product of K_2 and K_3? No that's prism graph. Octahedron is the 1-skeleton of regular octahedron, which is the dual of cube. Graph of cube is Q3 (3-cube) with 8 vertices degree 3. Dual gives octahedron with 6 vertices degree 4. Relationship: Octahedron graph is the complement of a perfect matching in K6. So adjacency matrix A = J - I - M where J all-ones, I identity, M is adjacency of matching (three disjoint edges). M has eigenvalues: 1 (mult 1) corresponding to vector (1,1,1,-1,-1,-1)?? Actually matching consists of three edges connecting pairs (1-2),(3-4),(5-6). Its adjacency matrix has eigenvalues: 1 (mult 1) for vector (1,1,1,1,1,1)?? Wait each vertex degree 1, so eigenvector (1,1,1,1,1,1) gives eigenvalue 1? Multiply M by that vector: each component sums neighbors: for vertex 1 neighbor 2 -> value 1, so result component =1. Similarly for vertex2 neighbor1 ->1. So yes eigenvalue 1 with multiplicity 1. There is also eigenvalue -1 with multiplicity 2? Let's compute spectrum of matching: It is basically three copies of K2. Spectrum of K2 is {1,-1}. For disjoint union, eigenvalues combine: each component contributes its eigenvalues. So overall eigenvalues: 1 (from each component) three times? Wait each K2 has eigenvalues 1 and -1. Disjoint union of three K2 gives eigenvalues: 1 appears three times, -1 appears three times. Yes.\n\nThus M eigenvalues: 1 (mult 3), -1 (mult 3).\n\nNow A = J - I - M. J has eigenvalues: n (=6) with eigenvector all-ones, and 0 with multiplicity n-1.\n\nCompute eigenvalues of A: For any eigenvector orthogonal to all-ones, J acts zero. So eigenvalue = -1 - \u03bb_M where \u03bb_M is eigenvalue of M on that subspace. Since M restricted to subspace orthogonal to all-ones has eigenvalues? M's eigenvectors include those within each pair. For vectors orthogonal to all-ones, we can consider components within each pair. For each pair (1,2): subspace spanned by (1,-1,0,0,0,0) eigenvalue -1 (since M acting swaps?). Actually M on that subspace has eigenvalue -1? Let's check: vector v=(1,-1,0...). Mv: for vertex1 neighbor2 value -1, so component = -1. For vertex2 neighbor1 value 1, so component = 1. So Mv = (-1,1,0...) = -v. So eigenvalue -1. Good. So for each pair, eigenvalue -1. Thus on subspace orthogonal to all-ones, M eigenvalue -1 (mult 3). Therefore A eigenvalue = -1 - (-1)=0 (mult 3). Good.\n\nFor eigenvector all-ones, J eigenvalue 6, I eigenvalue 1, M eigenvalue 1 (since all-ones is eigenvector of M with eigenvalue 1). So A eigenvalue = 6 -1 -1 =4 (mult 1). So far we have eigenvalues: 4 (1), 0 (3), and remaining eigenvalue? Sum of eigenvalues = trace(A)=0 (no loops). We have accounted 4+0*3=4, need total sum 0, so remaining eigenvalue = -4 (mult 1). Indeed matches expectation.\n\nThus eigenvalues of adjacency: 4,0 (mult 3), -4.\n\nTransition matrix P = (1/4) A. Hence eigenvalues \u03bc: 1, 0 (mult 3), -1 (mult 1). Good.\n\nNow probability of returning to start after t steps: p_t = (1/6) + (1/4) \u03a3_{i=2}^{6} \u03bc_i^t * \u03b1_i where \u03b1_i are coefficients determined by initial condition. But we can use formula for regular graph: p_t = (1/n) + (1/d) \u03a3_{i=2}^{n} (\u03bb_i/d)^t * (something). However we can compute using eigen-decomposition of P: P = U \u039b U^{-1}. Initial vector e_a expressed in eigenbasis: e_a = \u03a3_i c_i u_i where u_i are orthonormal eigenvectors. Then p_t = e_a^T P^t e_a = \u03a3_i c_i^2 \u03bc_i^t. Since eigenvectors orthonormal, c_i^2 = |\u27e8u_i, e_a\u27e9|^2. For regular graph, first eigenvector is all-ones normalized: u_1 = (1/\u221an) 1. Its coefficient c_1 = \u27e8u_1, e_a\u27e9 = 1/\u221an. So contribution from \u03bc_1=1 gives (1/n). For other eigenvectors orthogonal to all-ones, their inner product with e_a is some value. By symmetry, each such eigenvector has same absolute value? Not necessarily but due to symmetry of vertex labeling, the magnitude squared should be 1/n as well? Let's compute: For any eigenvector orthogonal to all-ones, its entries sum to 0. The squared norm of its projection onto e_a is (1/n) because average of squares? Actually since eigenvectors form orthonormal basis, the set of coordinates of e_a in this basis are orthonormal. The sum of squares of all coordinates = ||e_a||^2 =1. One coordinate is c_1^2 = 1/n. Therefore sum of squares of remaining coordinates = 1 - 1/n = (n-1)/n. Since there are n-1 eigenvectors left, the average of their squared magnitudes is ((n-1)/n)/(n-1) = 1/n. So each c_i^2 = 1/n for i\u22652 (if they are symmetric). Indeed due to symmetry, each non-principal eigenvector has same absolute value when projected onto any single basis vector. So we can take c_i^2 = 1/n for i=2..n.\n\nThus p_t = (1/n) + \u03a3_{i=2}^{n} (1/n) \u03bc_i^t = (1/n)[1 + \u03a3_{i=2}^{n} \u03bc_i^t].\n\nGreat! So for octahedron n=6, \u03bc list: 1,0 (mult3), -1 (mult1). So p_t = (1/6)[1 + 0^t*3 + (-1)^t]. Note 0^t = 0 for t>0, and 0^0 =1 (but t nonnegative integer). For t=0, we want probability =1 obviously. Plug t=0: 0^0 =1, (-1)^0=1 => p_0 = (1/6)[1+3*1+1]= (1/6)*5 =5/6? That's wrong; should be 1. Our formula fails at t=0 because 0^0 ambiguous. We'll treat separately: for t=0, p_0=1. For t>=1, 0^t=0. So p_t = (1/6)[1 + (-1)^t] for t \u22651. That gives p_t = (1/6)(1+(-1)^t). So for even t, (-1)^t=1 \u2192 p_t = (1/6)*(2)=1/3. For odd t, (-1)^t=-1 \u2192 p_t = (1/6)*(0)=0. Interesting: probability of being back at a after odd number of steps is zero (makes sense because bipartite? Octahedron graph is bipartite? Actually octahedron graph is bipartite? Each vertex connects to all except opposite, so partition into two sets of 3 opposite vertices? Yes opposite vertices not adjacent, so graph is bipartite with partitions size 3. Starting at a, after odd steps you must be in opposite partition, cannot be at a. So probability zero. After even steps, probability 1/3. Check t=2: Should be 1/3. Let's verify quickly: From a, move to neighbor (4 choices). After second step, probability to return to a? Compute: first step to neighbor b. From b, there are 4 neighbors: a plus three others (excluding opposite of b). Among them, only one leads back to a. So probability = (1/4)*(1/4)=1/16? Wait need to consider all possibilities: Starting at a, after 1 step you're at some neighbor (probability 1). After second step, chance to go back to a is 1/4 (since from neighbor, one of four edges goes back). So unconditional probability = 1/4. That's not 1/3. Something off. Let's recompute using our derived formula: p_2 = 1/3 \u22480.333, but direct reasoning gave 0.25. Let's examine error: In step 2, after moving to neighbor, you might have moved to a vertex that is adjacent to a (yes all neighbors are adjacent). From that neighbor, probability to go back to a is 1/4 indeed. So p_2 = 1/4. So our formula seems incorrect.\n\nWhere did mistake arise? The assumption that each non-principal eigenvector has equal squared projection onto e_a may be false. Let's compute actual coefficients.\n\nWe need eigenvectors of adjacency matrix of octahedron. Perhaps the eigenvectors correspond to representations of S_3? Might produce different weights.\n\nLet's compute using linear algebra: Choose labeling: vertices 1-6, with opposite pairs (1,4), (2,5), (3,6). Edges between all except opposite. So adjacency matrix A has zeros on diagonal, and zero entries for opposite pairs.\n\nWe can find eigenvectors:\n\n- All-ones vector u1.\n\n- Vectors constant on each opposite pair but alternating sign? Example v1 = (1,1,1,-1,-1,-1) corresponds to eigenvalue 4? Let's test: For vertex1, neighbors are all except 4 (opposite). So sum of neighbors values = 1+1+1+1+1 - (value at 4)?? Actually neighbors are vertices 2,3,5,6 (exclude 4). Their values: 1,1,-1,-1 =0. So A v1 at vertex1 =0, not 4. So not eigenvector.\n\nMaybe eigenvectors correspond to representation of S_3 permutations of pairs.\n\nLet's compute using Python mental? Could attempt analytic.\n\nAlternative method: Use recurrence relation for probability of being at a after t steps given symmetry classes: distance categories: vertices at distance 1 (neighbors) and distance 2 (opposite). Since graph bipartite, distances parity matters. Let p_t = Prob(at a). Let q_t = Prob(at a neighbor) (any specific neighbor?) Actually due to symmetry, all non-a vertices are either neighbor (distance 1) or opposite (distance 2). But note from a, opposite vertices are not adjacent, but reachable in 2 steps via neighbor.\n\nDefine states:\n- State A: at a.\n- State N: at a neighbor (any of the 4 neighbors).\n- State O: at opposite vertex (the unique vertex not adjacent to a). There's exactly 1 opposite vertex.\n\nBecause of symmetry, probability of being at any particular neighbor is same, and probability of being at opposite is same.\n\nLet p_t = P(A at time t)\nLet n_t = P(at a specific neighbor) (so total probability at any neighbor = 4 n_t)\nLet o_t = P(at opposite) (only one vertex)\n\nWe have p_t + 4 n_t + o_t =1.\n\nTransitions:\nFrom A (vertex a):\n- Moves to any neighbor uniformly: probability 1 (since degree 4). So after one step, you are definitely at a neighbor. So p_{t+1}=0? Wait we need recursion for next step.\n\nFrom neighbor N:\n- Adjacent vertices: a (1), the other three neighbors (excluding itself and opposite?) Actually neighbor N is adjacent to a and to the other three vertices that are not opposite of N? Let's label: Suppose a=1, neighbor b=2. Opposite of a is 4. Opposite of b is 5. Vertex 2 is adjacent to all except its opposite 5. So neighbors of 2 are {1,3,6,? actually 2 adjacent to 1,3,6,? also 2 adjacent to ? not 5, also not itself. The set of vertices excluding 2 and its opposite 5: {1,3,4,6}. So includes a (1), another neighbor (3), opposite of a (4), and opposite of b (5?) wait 5 is opposite of b, not adjacent. So neighbors are 1,3,4,6. So from neighbor, you can go back to a (prob 1/4), go to another neighbor (like 3) prob 1/4, go to opposite of a (4) prob 1/4, go to opposite of neighbor (6) prob 1/4. So from N, you can go to A, to another N (different neighbor), to O (opposite of a), or to O' (opposite of neighbor) which is also opposite of a? Actually opposite of neighbor is distinct from opposite of a unless neighbor is 