m a, you are at neighbor; staying would require self-loop which doesn't exist. So from neighbor, you never stay at same vertex.\n\nThus transition probabilities:\n\n- From neighbor to a: 1/4.\n- From neighbor to a specific other neighbor (there are 3 such): each 1/4? Actually total probability to go to a neighbor (any) is 3/4, distributed equally among the 3 other neighbors, each 1/4.\n\n- From neighbor to opposite of a (the unique opposite vertex): probability 1/4.\n\nThus from neighbor, you can go to opposite of a (the unique opposite vertex) with prob 1/4.\n\nFrom opposite of a (call O):\n- Its opposite is a (since opposite pairs). So O is adjacent to all vertices except a. So its neighbors are the 4 neighbors of a (i.e., the four vertices adjacent to a). So from O, you will move to a neighbor with probability 1 (since degree 4). So O_{t+1}=0, and each neighbor receives probability 1/4 from O.\n\nThus we have system:\n\nLet a_t, n_t, o_t as defined.\n\nThen:\n\na_{t+1} = (1/4) * (4 n_t) = n_t   because each neighbor contributes 1/4 to a.\n\nn_{t+1} = contributions:\n- From a: a_t * (1/4) (to each specific neighbor)\n- From neighbor: from each neighbor, probability to go to a specific other neighbor is 1/4, but there are 4 neighbors total, each sending probability 1/4 to each of the other three neighbors. For a particular neighbor j, incoming from other neighbors: each of the other 3 neighbors sends 1/4 to j. So total from neighbors = 3 * (1/4) * n_t (since each neighbor has probability n_t). Additionally, from opposite O: O_t * (1/4) (since O moves to each neighbor equally). So n_{t+1} = (1/4) a_t + (3/4) n_t + (1/4) o_t.\n\no_{t+1} = contributions:\n- From neighbor: each neighbor sends 1/4 to opposite of a. So total = 4 * (1/4) n_t = n_t.\n- From a: none.\n- From opposite O: none (goes to neighbors).\nThus o_{t+1} = n_t.\n\nNow we have recurrences:\n\na_{t+1} = n_t ...(1)\no_{t+1} = n_t ...(2)\nn_{t+1} = (1/4) a_t + (3/4) n_t + (1/4) o_t ...(3)\n\nInitial conditions: at t=0, a_0=1, n_0=0, o_0=0.\n\nWe can solve.\n\nFrom (1) and (2), a_{t+1}=o_{t+1}=n_t.\n\nThus a_t = o_t = n_{t-1} for t\u22651.\n\nPlug into (3):\n\nn_{t+1} = (1/4) a_t + (3/4) n_t + (1/4) o_t = (1/4) n_{t-1} + (3/4) n_t + (1/4) n_{t-1} = (3/4) n_t + (1/2) n_{t-1}.\n\nThus recurrence: n_{t+1} = (3/4) n_t + (1/2) n_{t-1}.\n\nWith initial values: n_0 =0, n_1 = ? From (1) a_1 = n_0 =0, so a_1=0. n_1 =? Using (3) with t=0: n_1 = (1/4) a_0 + (3/4) n_0 + (1/4) o_0 = (1/4)*1 +0+0 = 1/4. Good.\n\nNow compute n_2: n_2 = (3/4) n_1 + (1/2) n_0 = (3/4)*(1/4)+0 = 3/16.\n\nThen a_2 = n_1 = 1/4. Good matches earlier expectation! So p_2 = a_2 = 1/4.\n\nNow compute general solution of recurrence for n_t.\n\nRecurrence: n_{t+1} = (3/4) n_t + (1/2) n_{t-1}.\n\nCharacteristic equation: r^2 = (3/4) r + (1/2). Multiply by 4: 4 r^2 = 3 r + 2 => 4r^2 -3r -2 =0. Solve: discriminant \u0394 = 9 +32 =41. Roots: r = [3 \u00b1 sqrt(41)]/(8). So r1 = (3 + \u221a41)/8, r2 = (3 - \u221a41)/8.\n\nThus n_t = A r1^t + B r2^t.\n\nUse n_0=0, n_1=1/4.\n\nSet t=0: n_0 = A + B =0 => B = -A.\n\nt=1: n_1 = A r1 + B r2 = A r1 - A r2 = A (r1 - r2) = 1/4.\n\nThus A = (1/4) / (r1 - r2). Compute r1 - r2 = ( (3+\u221a41) - (3-\u221a41) ) /8 = (2\u221a41)/8 = \u221a41/4. So A = (1/4) / (\u221a41/4) = 1/\u221a41.\n\nThus A = 1/\u221a41, B = -1/\u221a41.\n\nTherefore n_t = (1/\u221a41)( r1^t - r2^t ).\n\nNow a_t = n_{t-1} for t\u22651. So a_t = (1/\u221a41)( r1^{t-1} - r2^{t-1} ). For t=0, a_0=1.\n\nThus probability p_t = a_t = \n- 1 if t=0,\n- (1/\u221a41)( r1^{t-1} - r2^{t-1} ) for t\u22651.\n\nWe can also express closed form using eigenvalues of transition matrix: eigenvalues are 1, \u03bb1, \u03bb2 where \u03bb1, \u03bb2 are roots of characteristic polynomial of transition matrix? Indeed eigenvalues of P are 1, \u03bb1, \u03bb2 where \u03bb1, \u03bb2 satisfy \u03bb^2 = (3/4) \u03bb + (1/2) after scaling? Actually transition matrix eigenvalues are 1, \u03bb1, \u03bb2 where \u03bb1, \u03bb2 are solutions of